 ],
 "literal": "Sea to consume not being lost, enemies do not dare to attack, / wearing hero's anklets on perfected feet, Kāri, your country indeed! / Sacrificial fire protecting Brahmins, that is theirs; / with undecaying wealth and victory-possessing army, / \"Among the three kings, may you become the strength/ally of one,\" / thus praising, the food/wealth given to your clan, / blessing and coming suppliants, that is theirs; / resembling the Northern Star in chastity, with gentle speech, / your wife's shoulders' measure, except for that, / \"yours\" as there is nothing, O you of great pride!",
 "translation": "O Kāri, who wears hero's anklets on your beautiful, perfected feet! Your country cannot be consumed by the sea, nor do your enemies dare to attack it. Yet, that country belongs to the Brahmins who tend the sacrificial fire. And the wealth given to your clan by those who praise you, saying, 'May you become the strength of one of the three great kings with unfailing wealth and victorious armies,' belongs to the suppliants who come blessing you. Other than the embrace of your wife, whose speech is gentle and whose chastity resembles the Northern Star, nothing is yours, O man of great pride!",
 "modern": "வீரக் கழல் அணிந்த அழகான பாதங்களை உடைய காரியே! உன்னுடைய நாடு கடலால் கொள்ளப்படாது; பகைவர்களும் அதனைக் கைப்பற்றத் துணிய மாட்டார்கள். ஆனால், அந்த நாடு வேள்வித் தீயை வளர்க்கும் அந்தணர்களுக்கே உரியது. அழியாத செல்வமும் வெற்றியும் கொண்ட சேனைகளை உடைய மூவேந்தர்களுள் ஒருவனுக்கு நீ துணையாக வேண்டும் என்று உன்னைப் புகழ்ந்து பாடிப் பெறும் செல்வங்கள் அனைத்தும், உன்னை வாழ்த்தி வரும் இரவலர்களுக்கே உரியன. வடமீனாகிய அருந்ததியைப் போன்ற கற்பும் மென்மையான மொழியும் கொண்ட உன் மனைவியின் தோள்களைத் தவிர, உன்னுடையது என்று சொல்லிக் கொள்ள உன்னிடம் வேறு எதுவும் இல்லை, பெருமிதம் உடையவனே!",
 "explanation": "The poet Kapilar is praising the chieftain Malaiyamāṉ Tirumuṭikkāri. He extols Kāri's extraordinary generosity and valor. The poet states that Kāri's land is secure from natural disasters like the sea and from enemy invasions. However, Kāri does not keep this land or his wealth for himself. The land is gifted to the Brahmins who perform sacred rituals, and the wealth he receives from the three great kings (Cēra, Cōḻa, and Pāṇṭiya) for his military alliance is entirely distributed to the bards and suppliants who visit him. The image of the Northern Star (Arundhati) represents the ultimate chastity and virtue of Kāri's wife. The contrast between Kāri's immense power and his absolute lack of personal possession highlights his supreme generosity (koṭai).",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
 "generated": "2026-11-26"
}